- op: create  # 2c7b
  at: '2026-05-30T05:41:10Z'  # 2c7b
  by: agent  # 2c7b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2c7b
  clock: 1  # 2c7b
  nonce: 2c7b  # 2c7b
  data:  # 2c7b
    kind: invariant  # 2c7b
    title: "Call graph does not traverse dispatch-primitive patterns, producing downstream false positives"  # 2c7b
    status: violated  # 2c7b
    priority: 1  # 2c7b
    parent:  # 2c7b
    tags:  # 2c7b
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # 2c7b
    - dogfood_integrated  # 2c7b
    - dogfood_severity_significant  # 2c7b
    isbefore: []  # 2c7b
    duplicate_of: []  # 2c7b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 2c7b
    pr_ref:  # 2c7b
    description: "The call graph used by `slice`, `features`, and `dead-code-maybe` misses edges for several patterns of indirect dispatch in framework-style code. Three distinct symptom areas trace to one underlying gap: edges for route-handler dispatch, closure-factory dispatch, and descriptor-protocol dispatch are not produced. Downstream consumers that BFS the call graph from seeds inherit the incompleteness as either silent false positives on dead-code detection, silently empty feature graphs for route-typed entrypoints, or missing reachability for the most-used dispatch primitives in the codebase.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) Dead-code-maybe default invocation reports 89.5% false-positive rate.** `hypergumbo dead-code-maybe --format json` with default `--seeds entrypoints` classifies 3855 of 4306 production functions as dead candidates on hypergumbo's own codebase. Switching to `--seeds all` drops the rate to 35.1% (1510 / 4306). Sampled false-positives from a random sample of 8 candidates: `TreeSitterAnalyzer.analyze` (base-class dispatch from every language analyzer), `cmd_install_rust_analyzer` (argparse subcommand dispatch from `main`), `TrackerSet.promote` (CLI command), `_build_tsconfig_alias_index` (helper called internally). Entrypoint detection covers 104 seeds; `--seeds all` covers 21,812. The gap is two-fold: argparse subcommand dispatch from `main()` does not connect `main → cmd_*` command functions, and ABC-style dispatch from a registry does not connect callers to virtual method bodies.\n\n**(2) Features synthesis emits empty edge sets for route entrypoints.** `hypergumbo features` emits 7 of 16 feature graphs with `edge_ids=[]`. The empty entries are all `:route`-typed entrypoints from `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/serve.py:426-432` (entries 8 through 14); the corresponding `:function`-typed entries 0-7 carry edges as expected, and entry 15 (the `/ws` WebSocket route) carries one edge. The features pass BFS-slices from each entrypoint, but `:route` symbols (which mark route registration lines, not handler bodies) carry no outbound edge to the handler `:function` symbol. The route → handler relationship was detected at the analyzer level (it appears in `entrypoints` and `usage_contexts`) but is missing as a graph edge that BFS can traverse.\n\n**(3) Dead-code-maybe flags closure-factory and descriptor patterns as dead even with `--seeds all`.** Specific false-positives include `register_analyzer.decorator`, `register_linker.decorator`, `register_def_use_extractor.decorator` (function-factory closures returned and used as decorators), and `Cache.delete`, `TrackerSet.config`, `Store.config` (dict-config-style attribute dispatch). Two structural gaps: the call-graph edge extraction does not model (a) nested closures returned from factory functions and subsequently used as decorators, and (b) descriptor-protocol dispatch (property/staticmethod-like attribute access).\n\n### Invariant\n\nThe call graph should resolve edges for each documented dispatch family — route → handler, closure-factory dispatch, descriptor-protocol dispatch — either through analyzer-emitted edges or through dedicated linkers. Consumers of the call graph (slice, features, dead-code-maybe, plus any downstream tooling that BFS-traverses it) should not need per-pattern workarounds, and `dead-code-maybe`'s default `--seeds entrypoints` invocation should be a usable tool rather than a near-100%-false-positive noise source.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects framework-style code broadly. Specific measured magnitudes on hypergumbo self-analysis: roughly half-empty feature graph for route entrypoints (7/16 empty), 89.5% false-positive rate on dead-code-maybe default invocation, and false-dead-code flags on the most-used dispatch primitives in the codebase (decorator factories used by every analyzer/linker registration).\n\n### Exposure\n\nBehavior-map call-graph edges (`calls`, `references`), slice output, features array, dead-code-maybe analysis output.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers using the call graph for any framework-aware analysis — route reachability, dead-code detection, refactoring impact — get systematically wrong results. The default `dead-code-maybe` invocation's 89.5% false-positive rate makes its output not directly actionable. Features-based queries on `:route` entrypoints return empty edge sets, breaking downstream consumers that expect non-empty graphs for emitted entrypoints.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Improve entrypoint detection to cover argparse subcommand dispatch (`main → cmd_*`) and ABC dispatch from registries; alternatively change the `--seeds` default from `entrypoints` to `all`.\n- Emit a route → handler edge at the analyzer or linker level so the features-BFS pass can traverse it. Detection already exists in entrypoints / usage_contexts; the edge just needs to land in the graph.\n- Extend call-graph edge extraction to model nested closures returned from factory functions used as decorators, and descriptor-protocol dispatch.\n- Add an \"indirect-dispatch coverage\" property test to the analyzer/linker test suite that asserts every documented dispatch family produces at least one traversable edge per fixture.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-lidot, INV-pavos, INV-tudum — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 2c7b
    fields:  # 2c7b
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-008e6d"  # 2c7b
      statement: "The call graph should resolve edges for each documented dispatch family — route→handler, closure-factory dispatch, descriptor-protocol dispatch. Either through analyzer-emitted edges or through dedicated linkers. Consumers of the call graph should not need per-pattern workarounds."  # 2c7b
      root_cause: "The call graph used by slicing, features, and dead-code analysis misses edges for several patterns of indirect dispatch. Route entrypoints emitted by web-framework linkers carry no outbound edge to the handler functions they dispatch to. Closure-factory patterns (functions returning closures that are used as decorators or callbacks) are not traversed. Descriptor-protocol patterns (property and staticmethod-like attribute dispatch) are not traversed. Downstream consumers inherit the incompleteness: a route-based forward slice from an entrypoint returns an empty edge set; a dead-code analysis using default seeds reports a very high false-positive rate because the most common dispatch primitives in framework-style code show up as unreachable."  # 2c7b
