  message: "Cross-ref backfill (consolidated, 2026-05-30): 11 active items outside the May-28 dogfood corpus chip at this integrated cluster's dispatch-primitive call-graph gap, each addressing a specific mechanism. (1) INV-miloj-tagum-garuk-jataf-lupas-ligis-gazaz-pimon — HTTP-linker URL extraction folding constant-composed URLs, with child Phases WI-mogan (string_interpolation), WI-lugag (printf_format), WI-vaguk (string_concat); (2) INV-nilud-zivag-jibak-danov-polak-sibam-fahif-katat — META on the 5-PR linkers/inherited_calls.py campaign (MRO walks for Java/Kotlin/Python/Ruby/PHP/etc.); (3) WI-kofaf-nanos-rukok-sabib-lahag-fopur-pokul-jahop — Go marshal callbacks caller-site detection; (4) WI-zimat-rafur-kiluh-kajof-bavud-soniz-vokud-mozim, WI-hijot-sosak-tupim-nanol-dubuv-kokiv-gahag-gubub, WI-hudam-bakim-birul-riban-vajis-siliz-tirug-valid, WI-fukut-godin-gidil-filif-dajob-kulik-luvud-total — Return-Type Registry phases (C++/Scala/Python/cross-linker integration); (5) WI-bimar-butif-fujus-jimag-nofuf-dalad-zibas-movok — Lua FFI LuaJIT metatype patterns. Each closure chips at this item's violation; this is not a child-parent relationship but a coverage map."  # 506d
- op: discuss  # 4eba
  at: '2026-06-02T16:12:14Z'  # 4eba
  by: agent  # 4eba
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4eba
  clock: 3  # 4eba
  nonce: 4eba  # 4eba
  message: "Pass-33 batch — dead-code-maybe FP characterization (4 findings: F33.21 dispatch-pattern catches only 1.2% / F33.22 --exclude-annotated weak filter / F33.23 99% Python-only / F33.24 cross_language_hits unused signal).\n\nINV-pohik's existing description quantifies the 89.6% FP rate and the --seeds entrypoints → all reduction (89.6% → 35.1%). Pass-33 extends the characterization with mechanism distribution and filter-effectiveness measurements.\n\n**F33.21 — Of 3891 default candidates, only 48 (1.2%) are class-name-detectable dispatch FPs.** Pass-33 classified all 3891 default `--seeds entrypoints` candidates by name pattern:\n```\n*Analyzer.analyze       : 40   (each registered via @register_analyzer decorator dispatch)\n*Extractor.extract      :  9   (each registered via @register_*_extractor decorator dispatch)\n*.decorator             :  3   (inner-returned functions of decorator factories)\n                          --\nTotal dispatch-name FPs : 48   (1.2% of 3891)\n\nBulk distribution:\nprivate_helper (`_*`)              : 2362 (60.7%)\nother (top-level + named functions): 1439 (37.0%)\nshort_method_probably_property     :   31 (0.8%)\n*Index.build                       :    3 (0.1%)\n*.render (Lit lifecycle)           :    3 (0.1%)\nbuild_tool_callback                :    1 (0.0%)\n```\nPractical implication: a class-name-pattern-matching FP demoter would catch only 1.2% of the false positives. The dominant cohort (60.7%) is private helpers — functions prefixed with `_` per Python convention — these are reachable from within their module but the BFS doesn't reach them because the module's entrypoint isn't in the seed set. Default `--seeds entrypoints` only includes 104 seeds; module-internal call graphs starting from non-seed roots are not traversed.\n\n**F33.22 — `--exclude-annotated` filter reduces candidates by only 11%** (3891 → 3459). Pass-33 ran `dead-code-maybe --exclude-annotated --format json` and observed:\n```\ndefault                : 3891 dead candidates\n--exclude-annotated    : 3459 dead candidates (reduction: 432, 11.1%)\n--seeds all            : 1522 dead candidates (reduction: 2369, 60.9%)\n```\nThe annotation filter catches only the cohort whose `decorators` field is populated — does not catch base-class dispatch, registry dispatch via class-name matching, or framework lifecycle methods. `--seeds all` is **5.5× more effective** at FP reduction than `--exclude-annotated`. The annotation filter is plausibly correct for its narrow scope but is the wrong knob to recommend as the FP mitigation; the seeds choice is.\n\n**F33.23 — 99.0% of dead candidates are Python despite substrate having 17 languages with nodes.** Per-language distribution:\n```\npython    : 3854  (99.0%)\ntypescript:   17  (0.4%)\nNone      :   17  (0.4%)  -- nodes with no language field\nbash      :    3  (0.1%)\nothers    :    0\n```\nSubstrate has csharp, go, java, rust, swift, solidity, sql, etc. with nodes, but their symbols don't appear in the dead-candidate set. Either: (a) the \"Production functions: 4342\" count includes only Python (and `--seeds entrypoints` only reaches Python), (b) non-Python analyzers don't populate the `is_test_file` / `is_exported` flags that the dead-code-maybe filter uses, (c) entrypoints for non-Python are detected (the 104 seeds include shell_script and library_export entries) but their reachability traversal terminates immediately. Pass-32 F32.4 showed many non-Python analyzers run but emit zero nodes; F33.23 surfaces the corollary at the dead-code-maybe layer: the FP signal is effectively Python-only.\n\n**F33.24 — cross_language_hits is a populated signal but unused as FP demoter.** Each dead candidate record carries a `cross_language_hits` field. Distribution:\n```\ncandidates with cross_language_hits > 0 : 269\nmax cross_language_hits                 : 135\nTop by CLH (samples):\n  _AxisSpec.name              : 135\n  ItemDetail.render           :  94  (Lit component lifecycle)\n  ItemList.render             :  94  (Lit component lifecycle)\n  TrackerApp.render           :  94  (Lit component lifecycle)\n  NameMatcher.match           :  80\n  transform                   :  69  (vite config callback)\n  Cache.delete                :  63\n  register_analyzer.decorator :  61  (decorator wrapper)\n```\nA symbol with `cross_language_hits=94` has its name matched 94 times in other-language source contexts. For the 3 Lit `.render` methods, this is overwhelming evidence the symbol is reachable via the cross-language matching that INV-pohik's structural model already covers. **The signal is computed and stored on every candidate but is not used as an FP demotion criterion.** Demoting candidates with `cross_language_hits >= N` (for some threshold) would mechanically catch this cohort. Pass-33 fix-path candidate: add `--exclude-cross-language-matches` flag, default-off, that demotes candidates whose CLH exceeds a threshold.\n\n**Aggregate FP-reduction stack** (Pass-33 measured cumulatively):\n```\nDefault (--seeds entrypoints)  : 3891  /  4342  =  89.6% dead\n--exclude-annotated            : 3459  /  4342  =  79.7% dead  (≈10pp reduction)\n--seeds all                    : 1522  /  4342  =  35.1% dead  (≈55pp reduction)\nHypothetical --seeds all + --exclude-annotated + --exclude-cross-language-matches:\n    estimated based on F33.24 — would further reduce by ≈3-7pp on top of seeds=all.\n```\nMechanical filters (cross_language_hits, base-class detection, registry-name matching) are necessary but not sufficient. The dominant signal is seeds choice. INV-pohik fix-path candidate: change default `dead-code-maybe` invocation to `--seeds all` and warn explicitly when defaulting to entrypoints. The current default produces a 90% FP rate which has been the dogfood-corpus framing all along; users seeing the default output get an unusable signal.\n\n**Probe artifact.** `/tmp/p33_outputD2.log` (D.2 classification, D.3 confirmed-FP count, D.5 language distribution, D.6 CLH distribution, D.7 seeds=all comparison, D.8 --exclude-annotated comparison).\n\n**No status change.** Pass-33 supplies a mechanism-distribution + filter-effectiveness characterization to INV-pohik's existing description. Concrete fix-path candidates: (a) change default to `--seeds all`; (b) wire `cross_language_hits` as FP demoter; (c) add private-helper-vs-internal-call detection (the 60.7% bulk cohort)."  # 4eba
