  target_nonce: 4eba  # 4435
  new_text: "Pass-33 batch — dead-code-maybe FP characterization (F33.23 99% Python-only migrated to WI-tosul; F33.24 cross_language_hits unused migrated to WI-gavub per fold-audit Phase 2b. F33.21 + F33.22 retained as INV-pohik quantification extensions).\n\nINV-pohik's existing description quantifies the 89.6% FP rate and the --seeds entrypoints → all reduction (89.6% → 35.1%). Pass-33 extends the characterization with mechanism distribution and filter-effectiveness measurements.\n\n**F33.21 — Of 3891 default candidates, only 48 (1.2%) are class-name-detectable dispatch FPs.** Pass-33 classified all 3891 default `--seeds entrypoints` candidates by name pattern:\n```\n*Analyzer.analyze       : 40   (each registered via @register_analyzer decorator dispatch)\n*Extractor.extract      :  9   (each registered via @register_*_extractor decorator dispatch)\n*.decorator             :  3   (inner-returned functions of decorator factories)\n                          --\nTotal dispatch-name FPs : 48   (1.2% of 3891)\n\nBulk distribution:\nprivate_helper (`_*`)              : 2362 (60.7%)\nother (top-level + named functions): 1439 (37.0%)\nshort_method_probably_property     :   31 (0.8%)\n*Index.build                       :    3 (0.1%)\n*.render (Lit lifecycle)           :    3 (0.1%)\nbuild_tool_callback                :    1 (0.0%)\n```\nPractical implication: a class-name-pattern-matching FP demoter would catch only 1.2% of the false positives. The dominant cohort (60.7%) is private helpers — functions prefixed with `_` per Python convention — these are reachable from within their module but the BFS doesn't reach them because the module's entrypoint isn't in the seed set. Default `--seeds entrypoints` only includes 104 seeds; module-internal call graphs starting from non-seed roots are not traversed.\n\n**F33.22 — `--exclude-annotated` filter reduces candidates by only 11%** (3891 → 3459). Pass-33 ran `dead-code-maybe --exclude-annotated --format json` and observed:\n```\ndefault                : 3891 dead candidates\n--exclude-annotated    : 3459 dead candidates (reduction: 432, 11.1%)\n--seeds all            : 1522 dead candidates (reduction: 2369, 60.9%)\n```\nThe annotation filter catches only the cohort whose `decorators` field is populated — does not catch base-class dispatch, registry dispatch via class-name matching, or framework lifecycle methods. `--seeds all` is **5.5× more effective** at FP reduction than `--exclude-annotated`. The annotation filter is plausibly correct for its narrow scope but is the wrong knob to recommend as the FP mitigation; the seeds choice is.\n\n**Aggregate FP-reduction stack** (Pass-33 measured cumulatively):\n```\nDefault (--seeds entrypoints)  : 3891  /  4342  =  89.6% dead\n--exclude-annotated            : 3459  /  4342  =  79.7% dead  (≈10pp reduction)\n--seeds all                    : 1522  /  4342  =  35.1% dead  (≈55pp reduction)\n```\nMechanical filters (cross_language_hits per WI-gavub, base-class detection, registry-name matching) are necessary but not sufficient. The dominant signal is seeds choice. INV-pohik fix-path candidate: change default `dead-code-maybe` invocation to `--seeds all` and warn explicitly when defaulting to entrypoints. The current default produces a 90% FP rate which has been the dogfood-corpus framing all along; users seeing the default output get an unusable signal.\n\n**Probe artifact.** `/tmp/p33_outputD2.log` (D.2 classification, D.3 confirmed-FP count, D.7 seeds=all comparison, D.8 --exclude-annotated comparison).\n\nNo status change. Pass-33 supplies a mechanism-distribution + filter-effectiveness characterization to INV-pohik's existing description."  # 4435
  reason: "fold-audit Phase 2b: F33.23 to WI-tosul, F33.24 to WI-gavub; F33.21+22 kept"  # 4435
